INT. HARLOW & SONS DELIVERY WAREHOUSE - NIGHT

Rain on the tin roof. Steady. Endless.

Rows of parcels stacked to the rafters. A forklift, parked. Sodium lights buzzing orange.

MAYA (17, hoodie, lanyard that says TEMP) scans boxes with a handheld gun. BEEP. BEEP. BEEP.

Her phone buzzes on a pallet. She grabs it.

ON PHONE: omg r u still at work
ON PHONE: u said u'd be done by 10

MAYA
(typing, muttering)
It's literally ten-fifteen, Bee. Chill.

She thumbs a reply. Sends.

Quiet.

Cold concrete. Wet footprints leading in from the loading door.

JOSS (18, delivery vest, headphones around his neck) wheels a cage trolley around the corner.

JOSS
You're still here, Maya?

MAYA
Last row. Then I'm out.

JOSS
Cool, cool. Me too.

He parks the trolley. Sits on a crate. Scrolls his phone.

Silence.

Rain.

MAYA
You been on shift since six, right? Don't you wanna go home?

JOSS
Home's boring. Dad's watching the match.

MAYA
Fair.

She laughs. He doesn't look up.

A drip lands in a bucket. Plink.

Nothing.

MAYA (CONT'D)
Joss. Why are you holding your phone like that?

JOSS
Like what?

MAYA
Like it's a grenade.

He turns the screen toward her. A group chat. Her name at the top. A photo of her, at the till, from this afternoon.

JOSS
Bee sent me this. Asked me to keep you here.

Her phone. Buzzing. Buzzing. Buzzing.

BEE (O.S.)
(through the loading door, soft)
Maya? Surprise.

Maya turns. A silhouette in the rain. A birthday cake, candles lit, flickering.

BEE (CONT'D)
Happy seventeenth, idiot.

MAYA
You absolute --

She is laughing. Crying. Both.

Candlelight. Rain. Home.
